harder so rates of depression rates of  anxiety substantially higher and  suicidal thoughts so the crisis that we  had before has been accelerated by the  pandemic need now we were telling  parents parents earlier in the show that  we will let them know the signs they  should look out for so what are some of  those signs well some of the signs are  so for so if you have a child like  peyton  and he comes to you and says here's what  i'm dealing with it will be when it's  least convenient for you you'll be  working on a project you'll be you know  it's the day before your uh tax  admissions are due stop what you're  doing because that's an affirmation of  all the love and the relationship  between you and your child when they  come to you it's a moment of tremendous  vulnerability  so i'm struggling i'm not feeling safe  that's a big moment so take advantage of  that moment if you happen to be in a  situation like not all kids vocalize not  all kids do that and that's why it's so  impressive what peyton was able to do  there's still a lot of shame there's  still a lot of a sense that you  shouldn't have this experience  these are ordinary common treatable  conditions and we just have to move past  this idea that they people have to  suffer in science i was reading that one  of the things that a parent should look  for is  losing interest in things but in  particular losing interest in friends  losing interest in friends because  remember back to the idea of development  they want to be with their friends yeah  right they're trying to find an identity  that's separate from us as much as we  might not always love that so loss of  interest in friends sleep  changes changes in grades  irritability and anger can be a sign of  depression it's not always sadness and  withdrawal  isolation is a concern  and i feel like there's a constellation  of things that are fairly non-specific  but when you start to add them up an  evaluation might be a good idea i  thought it was interesting that the  mother said which is what most parents  would say what did i do what did i not  do  so what should we do and what should we  not do well first of all it's not your  fault and i think it's hard not to feel  it it's hard not to feel it isn't it  yeah because we're so invested in doing  the right thing for our kids and we take  credit for their success wait a minute  uh but the truth is  one in four one in five and in the  pandemic even higher numbers of  teenagers are experiencing depression  anxiety and suicidal thoughts so what  are we supposed to do what are we  supposed to do right so first of all the  good and the bad yeah keep the  conversation going  it's okay to have this it's okay to be  talking about this experience  that's important to keep the  conversation going what if they say i  don't want to talk about it everything's  fine i don't want to talk about it  everything's fine so you know this is my  experience pressing straight on it  you know you look depressed you need  help  may not be your best strategy because a  person can say oh actually that's not  really where i want to so find something  that they see that you also see  i can't sleep  none of my friends want to talk to me  focus on that in the venn diagram of  what you see which is i'm worried you  might be depressed and the venn diagram  of what they can see i'm not sleeping  and my friends don't want to talk to me  focus on the purple right in the middle  and say well let's talk about the sleep  or let's talk about the friends and  let's work off of that because that  matters to you  another strategy is to get a  pediatrician involved most people love  their pediatricians right pediatricians  are basically wonderful human beings  yeah and they're the under they are  the heroes of the american mental health  system they're the one of the backbones  of the american mental health system  yeah and so say you know because you're  having trouble sleeping do you mind if  we call dr smith  like why i like dr smith well this is  part of pediatricians no for sure mental  health is a big part of pediatric work  so if they won't talk to you  see if you can get another person to  engage in the conversation and if you  get a call from a friend who's worried  about your child that's an urgent  situation take it seriously take it  seriously no doubt now i i hear you and  what i'm hearing as a parent is we need  to be hyper aware of what was going on  in the home but also it's not our fault  it's not our fault we should be hyper  aware you know it's complicated isn't it  because you have to be fully engaged no  doubt not your fault we have to go dr  kenworth thank you dr duckworth i  appreciate you so much um if you are  someone who's seeking mental health  resources you can call the national  alliance on mental illness helpline  that's  1-800-950-6264 in a crisis text nami to  741-741 we'll be right back

